// Knuckles character data — the sprite trio the shared player frame reads out of a
// CharacterDef (mappings + DPLC + art), plus the two CRAM line-0 palettes the
// roster's per-character palette swap points at.
//
// ONE set, unlike Tails: Knuckles is a single sprite. He has no appendage object,
// so there is no second mappings/DPLC/art trio and no second VRAM window.
//
// PROVENANCE: stock Sonic 3 & Knuckles, extracted and converted to Aeon's sprite
// formats by games/sonic4/data/characters_staging/gen_characters.py (that
// directory's README carries the exact skdisasm sources), and shipped to the paths
// below by that generator's own `ship` list.
//
// HE SHIPS THE RAW DPLC PAIR, NOT THE `_opt` PAIR — the one place his data
// deliberately differs from Sonic's and Tails'. The contiguous ("optimized")
// rearrange copies every frame's tiles in full, duplicates included, so it makes
// the art strictly LARGER: 4383 tiles against the raw 4092. A DPLC entry's
// tile_start field is only TWELVE bits, so the contiguous form is not
// addressable and 25 of its entries across frames 234-250 silently wrapped to
// low tile indices — those frames DMA'd early-frame art. `write_dplc` now refuses
// to emit it (see the tile_start assert there), and the raw pair is both correct
// and 8,702 bytes smaller. THE BARE "(max N)" THAT USED TO CLOSE THIS SENTENCE IS
// GONE ON PURPOSE: the ceiling is now DPLC_ADDRESSABLE_TILES in
// engine/objects/dplc.emp and it is CHECKED against this file's sheet, by the
// ensure beside the VRAM wall below. The paragraph is kept because it is the only
// record of WHY Knuckles ships the raw pair while Sonic and Tails ship `_opt` —
// history a machine check cannot carry — but it no longer restates the number, so
// it cannot drift away from it. Its only cost is up to 5 DPLC entries per frame
// instead of 2 — genuinely inside DMA_IMPORTANT_SLOTS, and now ENFORCED by the
// dplc_peak_entries guard below rather than asserted here. Moving the same 29
// tiles either way. See docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md.
//
// The "(DPLC_Sonic carries up to 13)" that used to close this paragraph as
// reassurance was the opposite of reassuring: 13 > DMA_IMPORTANT_SLOTS. Sonic is
// the module that is over budget, not the precedent that makes 5 safe — see the
// ratchet in games/sonic4/data/collision/collision_data.emp.
//
// TWO PALETTES, and this is the structural reason Knuckles needed a per-character
// palette at all. Tails' art was fixable by a lossless index PERMUTATION into our
// existing CRAM line 0, because he uses only colours we already carry. Knuckles
// uses S3K index 5 ($0080) for 3,272 pixels and our line 0 does not carry that
// colour at any index, so no permutation is lossless for him. His art therefore
// stays in S3K's index order and the PALETTE moves instead — exactly what S3K
// itself does (it swaps Pal_Knuckles into CRAM line 0 for him).
module games.sonic4.knuckles_data in knuckles_data

// The character DPLC window's DECLARED EXTENT — the VRAM budget the peak guard
// below is stated against. Sole authority is games.sonic4.constants, whose
// vram-map block emits it from games/sonic4/vram.toml beside the window's base.
use games.sonic4.constants.{VRAM_TEST_SONIC_TILES}
use engine.constants.{TILE_SIZE, DMA_IMPORTANT_SLOTS}
use engine.objects.dplc.{dplc_peak_tiles, dplc_peak_entries, DPLC_ENTRY_RESERVE,
                         DPLC_ADDRESSABLE_TILES, offset_table_frames,
                         empty_frame_mismatches}

const _map_knux  = embed("games/sonic4/data/mappings/knuckles.bin")
const _dplc_knux = embed("games/sonic4/data/dplc/knuckles.bin")
const _art_knux  = embed("art/optimized/characters/knuckles.bin")

// The two CRAM line-0 images the roster swaps between. 32 bytes each = one
// 16-entry line. `Pal_SonicTails` is the SAME file act_assets.emp loads at level
// init, embedded here rather than referenced cross-module so a character record
// never has to name a per-act level asset (BGND_Palette lives in the OJZ act
// module and will be retired when the act set grows). 32 bytes of duplication
// buys the character data being self-contained.
const _pal_sonic_tails = embed("art/palettes/SonicAndTails.bin")
const _pal_knux        = embed("art/palettes/knuckles.bin")

// The Map_/DPLC_ frame indices are signed word-offset tables (AnimateSprite and
// Perform_DPLC both index them as `frame*2` into a word table of offsets FROM the
// table head), so each blob must fit the signed range — the same comptime wall
// collision_data.emp puts on the Sonic pair and tails_data.emp on Tails'.
ensure(_map_knux.len <= $7FFF, "Map_Knuckles exceeds signed word-offset range")
ensure(_dplc_knux.len <= $7FFF, "DPLC_Knuckles exceeds signed word-offset range")

// Every blob even-length, so the labels after them stay word-aligned with no
// inter-blob padding.
ensure((_map_knux.len & 1) == 0 && (_dplc_knux.len & 1) == 0 && (_art_knux.len & 1) == 0,
       "a Knuckles blob is odd-length — the label after it would land on an odd address")

// -----------------------------------------------
// MAP <-> DPLC FRAME-COUNT BINDING (LS-9, 2026-09-07). One `mapping_frame` byte
// indexes both tables, so a table with fewer frames than its partner is read PAST
// ITS END and a byte of the blob after it is followed as a frame offset.
//
// This pair carries the tree's one recorded instance of a DPLC shipped WRONG
// against its art (the `_opt` wrap recorded further down this file), which is the
// argument for stating the cheap structural facts about it rather than trusting
// the producer.
// -----------------------------------------------
ensure(offset_table_frames(_map_knux) == offset_table_frames(_dplc_knux),
       "Map_Knuckles declares {offset_table_frames(_map_knux)} frames and DPLC_Knuckles {offset_table_frames(_dplc_knux)} — one mapping_frame byte indexes BOTH, so the shorter table is read past its end. Re-export the PAIR, never one half. DOES NOT COVER: whether either count matches what Ani_Knuckles' frame bytes reach (AnimateSprite bounds no frame byte — LS-9a), nor whether corresponding frames describe the same art — which is the class the `_opt` tile_start wrap belonged to and this guard would NOT have caught")
ensure(empty_frame_mismatches(_map_knux, _dplc_knux,
                              offset_table_frames(_map_knux)) == 0,
       "{empty_frame_mismatches(_map_knux, _dplc_knux, offset_table_frames(_map_knux))} Knuckles frame(s) disagree about being empty — a drawn frame whose DPLC loads nothing renders whatever the character window last held, and a loaded frame that draws nothing spends Important queue slots on unreferenced tiles. DOES NOT COVER: whether a NON-empty pair agrees on how MUCH (both counts are compared only against zero), nor the frame count (the guard above)")

// Art is whole 32-byte tiles by construction (uncompressed 4bpp); a truncated
// embed would otherwise surface only as garbage in a frame's last DMA.
ensure((_art_knux.len % TILE_SIZE) == 0, "Art_Knuckles is not a whole number of tiles")

// A CRAM line is exactly 16 entries. Both palettes are read as a fixed 32-byte
// copy into Palette_Buffer by Player_RefreshPhysics, so a short file would copy
// whatever followed it into CRAM and a long one would silently ignore the tail.
ensure(_pal_sonic_tails.len == 32, "Pal_SonicTails must be exactly one 16-entry CRAM line (32 bytes)")
ensure(_pal_knux.len == 32, "Pal_Knuckles must be exactly one 16-entry CRAM line (32 bytes)")

// -----------------------------------------------
// SHARED LINE-0 SLOTS — the convention that keeps every shared sprite the same colour
// whichever character is active. Two user-reported bugs live here: the effect dust
// rendering RED as Knuckles (2026-08-12) and the red spring's base rendering RED/ORANGE
// as Knuckles (2026-09-09).
//
// CRAM line 0 is the PER-CHARACTER line: Player_RefreshPhysics copies the active
// CharacterDef.cd_palette over it (player_common.emp, `lea Palette_Buffer, a2` +
// `ori.b #1, Palette_Dirty`). So any art drawn with palette 0 renders through whichever
// character's line is loaded, and a slot the two palettes disagree about recolours that
// art on a character swap. The line-0 shared drawers and the indices they use:
//
//   effect dust (DustPuff / DustSpindash)     0, 4, 6, 7          authored gray
//   insta-shield (Art_InstaShield)            0, 6, 7, 8
//   spring (Art_Spring, test_solid.emp)       0, 1, 6, 7, 8, 9, 12, 13
//
// THE FIX, and it is S3K's own: stock S3K's Pal_SonicTails and Pal_Knuckles are identical
// at 13 of 16 slots and differ only at 2/3/4 — which is why S3K can draw its spring with
// `make_art_tile($4A4,0,0)`, the character's own line. We had lost that (our Sonic line is
// sonic_hack's index order, our Knuckles line was S3K's), so the two agreed at only 7
// slots. gen_characters.py's KNUCKLES_TO_AEON now re-indexes his art AND his palette into
// our order together — a pure relabel, every Knuckles pixel keeps its exact colour — and
// the two lines agree at 12 of 16, differing only at the four CHARACTER-SPECIFIC slots
// 2/3/5/9. HUD/rings are on line 1 (unaffected); the Tails appendage rides line 0 only
// while Tails is active, so it needs no Knuckles alignment.
//
// THE BUILD-TIME ENFORCEMENT below is in two parts: the general agreement count, and the
// dust's three slots pinned to their AUTHORED gray values (not merely to each other).
// idx N = CRAM word at bytes 2N..2N+1 (big-endian).
//
// ⚠ SLOT 9 IS THE KNOWN HOLE, and it is why the spring is not fully fixed. Our line 0
// carries $0444 there; Knuckles has no $0444, so his leftover colour ($0080, green) lands
// there by elimination — the destination is FORCED, since the other three free slots are
// pinned by Sonic's own colours through the derived S3K->Aeon table. The spring's 184
// index-9 pixels (coil mid-tone) still recolour under him. Closing it costs a colour from
// one side or the other, i.e. a LOOK decision: docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md "Spring index 9".
// -----------------------------------------------

// The slots the two shipped character lines are RULED to differ at. Not "the slots they
// happen to differ at" — this is the whole invariant, and a slot leaving the agreeing set
// silently recolours a shared sprite for one character.
// SELF-CONTAINED by rule, not by taste: a comptime fn's free names resolve at the CALL
// SITE, module siblings included (docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §2, amended 2026-09-07 — a helper
// calling a helper silently computed a wrong count there). So this one calls nothing and
// spells the CRAM word extraction inline. 16 slots, byte pairs, big-endian.
comptime fn line0_mismatches(a: Data, b: Data) -> int {
    comptime var n = 0
    for i in 0..16 {
        comptime var wa = (a[i << 1] << 8) | a[(i << 1) + 1]
        comptime var wb = (b[i << 1] << 8) | b[(i << 1) + 1]
        if wa != wb { n = n + 1 }
    }
    return n
}

// Four: 2/3/5 (Sonic's blues vs Knuckles' reds) and 9 (the forced hole above). More than
// four means a slot left the agreeing set; fewer means the hole closed and this comment,
// the DEFERRED_WORK entry and the spring's own guard are all stale.
ensure(line0_mismatches(_pal_sonic_tails, _pal_knux) == 4,
       "Pal_SonicTails and Pal_Knuckles differ at {line0_mismatches(_pal_sonic_tails, _pal_knux)} line-0 slots, not the ruled 4 (2/3/5 character hues + 9, the forced $0444/$0080 hole). Line 0 is the per-character line, so the AGREEING slots are what make shared line-0 art (effect dust, insta-shield, spring) render the same under either character — a slot leaving that set is the 2026-08-12 red-dust / 2026-09-09 red-spring bug returning. Regenerate with games/sonic4/data/characters_staging/gen_characters.py, which rules the permutation. DOES NOT COVER: WHICH slots differ (a swap of two disagreements holds the count), nor whether any shared art actually draws through them — the spring states its own per-index guard in games/sonic4/objects/test_solid.emp")
ensure(((_pal_sonic_tails[18] << 8) | _pal_sonic_tails[19]) != ((_pal_knux[18] << 8) | _pal_knux[19]),
       "slot 9 now AGREES between the two character lines — the forced $0444/$0080 hole is closed, so the spring's index-9 caveat above, its guard in test_solid.emp and the docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md 'Spring index 9' entry are all stale. Delete them and tighten the mismatch count to 3.")

// PINNED TO THE AUTHORED GRAYS, not merely to each other. These used to assert
// only that Pal_Knuckles AGREED with Pal_SonicTails at 4/6/7 — which is not the
// property the fix secured. Re-export BOTH palettes with those slots recoloured in
// lockstep and all three agreement asserts pass while the skid/spindash/slide dust
// turns that colour for every character, i.e. exactly the bug (dust rendering RED
// as Knuckles) coming back with a green build. The dust art is authored gray, so
// the values are the invariant and belong here as literals.
//
// Roster-scoped by construction: a fourth character's palette adds three lines
// against the SAME constants, not against whichever palette happened to ship first.
const DUST_GRAY_IDX4 = $0ECC
const DUST_GRAY_IDX6 = $0EEE    // the dust's dominant colour
const DUST_GRAY_IDX7 = $0CAA

ensure((_pal_sonic_tails[8] << 8 | _pal_sonic_tails[9]) == DUST_GRAY_IDX4,
       "Pal_SonicTails moved off the authored effect-dust gray at idx4 — the shared dust would recolour")
ensure((_pal_sonic_tails[12] << 8 | _pal_sonic_tails[13]) == DUST_GRAY_IDX6,
       "Pal_SonicTails moved off the authored effect-dust gray at idx6 — the shared dust would recolour")
ensure((_pal_sonic_tails[14] << 8 | _pal_sonic_tails[15]) == DUST_GRAY_IDX7,
       "Pal_SonicTails moved off the authored effect-dust gray at idx7 — the shared dust would recolour")
ensure((_pal_knux[8] << 8 | _pal_knux[9]) == DUST_GRAY_IDX4,
       "Pal_Knuckles moved off the authored effect-dust gray at idx4 — the shared dust would recolour")
ensure((_pal_knux[12] << 8 | _pal_knux[13]) == DUST_GRAY_IDX6,
       "Pal_Knuckles moved off the authored effect-dust gray at idx6 — the shared dust would recolour")
ensure((_pal_knux[14] << 8 | _pal_knux[15]) == DUST_GRAY_IDX7,
       "Pal_Knuckles moved off the authored effect-dust gray at idx7 — the shared dust would recolour")

// -----------------------------------------------
// THE VRAM WALL — the peak comes from the DATA, the ceiling from the MAP.
//
// Knuckles shares the single character DPLC window with Sonic and Tails (only one
// character is resident at a time), so his file-wide peak has to clear the same
// wall theirs does. Measured 29 tiles into the 32-tile window: the same peak
// Sonic has, and the tightest of the three.
//
// ⚠ THE CEILING USED TO BE THE NEIGHBOUR'S BASE (VRAM_TEST_OBJ), under a comment
// claiming that moving that neighbour re-checked this automatically (LS-4, fixed
// 2026-09-07). It is now `character_window`'s own declared extent, so this guard
// names no other region. A neighbour still cannot take tiles from this window
// unnoticed: gen_vram_map.py enforces overlap and full 0..2047 coverage, so any
// such take shows up as this region's `tiles` dropping — which lands here red.
//
// NOTE this is measured on the RAW DPLC, where a frame's tiles arrive as up to 5
// entries. dplc_peak_tiles sums per frame, so it measures the VRAM footprint
// (29 tiles) and not the entry count — which is the right quantity for this wall.
// -----------------------------------------------
ensure(dplc_peak_tiles(_dplc_knux) <= VRAM_TEST_SONIC_TILES,
       "Knuckles' peak DPLC frame is {dplc_peak_tiles(_dplc_knux)} tiles and overruns the {VRAM_TEST_SONIC_TILES}-tile character VRAM window — re-page the art, or grow `character_window` in games/sonic4/vram.toml (raise `tiles`, hold `base`) and regenerate. DOES NOT COVER: the queue-slot cost of that frame (the entries guard below), the straddle split neither ensure can see, nor Sonic and Tails, who share this window and carry their own copies in collision_data.emp and tails_data.emp — a fix here fixes ONE of three")

// DPLC_PEAK_TILES_KNUCKLES — the second route for tools/dma_defer_headroom.py's pinned
// per-frame DPLC byte peak. Zero ROM bytes; one EQU row the tool reads back so
// its Python parse of this blob can be CONTRADICTED by sigil's own. The full
// contract, and what it still cannot say, is in collision_data.emp beside
// DPLC_PEAK_TILES_SONIC.
pub equ DPLC_PEAK_TILES_KNUCKLES = dplc_peak_tiles(_dplc_knux)

// The OTHER budget the note above disclaims: entries are Important queue SLOTS,
// one enqueue each. Knuckles' raw table peaks at 5, comfortably inside the real
// bound, so this is a true guard rather than a ratchet.
ensure(dplc_peak_entries(_dplc_knux) + DPLC_ENTRY_RESERVE <= DMA_IMPORTANT_SLOTS,
       "Knuckles' peak DPLC frame costs more Important queue slots than the budget leaves — re-page the art or raise DMA_IMPORTANT_SLOTS")

// -----------------------------------------------
// THE 12-BIT tile_start CEILING — the machine check the header paragraph used to
// be, and THE TIGHTEST BOUND ON ANY SHEET IN THIS TREE.
//
// 4,092 tiles against DPLC_ADDRESSABLE_TILES: FOUR TILES of headroom, 128 bytes.
// Sonic has 1,050 and Tails 461. Any Knuckles re-export that adds five tiles
// fails the build — which is the guard working, not the guard being wrong: the raw
// pair ships precisely because the contiguous one was 4,383 and wrapped.
//
// The check is on the SHEET, not on the table, and that is not an approximation of
// something better — the shipped table's tile_start field is 12 bits wide, so its
// largest value is <= 4,095 for every possible input and an ensure over it could
// never fire. The wrap happens in the producer, before the file exists. Full
// reasoning at the constants block in engine/objects/dplc.emp.
//
// (Of his 4,092 tiles the DPLC's furthest reach is 4,076, so 16 tail tiles are
// unreferenced. The bound is stated on the sheet anyway: a producer may start a run
// at any tile it likes, so only "every tile is nameable" is safe against a re-layout.)
// -----------------------------------------------
ensure(_art_knux.len / TILE_SIZE <= DPLC_ADDRESSABLE_TILES,
       "Art_Knuckles is {_art_knux.len / TILE_SIZE} tiles; a DPLC entry's tile_start can only name 0..{DPLC_ADDRESSABLE_TILES - 1}, so the tiles above that would be addressed by a WRAPPED index and load early-frame art — this is the 4,383-tile `_opt` defect the module header records, arriving through the raw pair. Re-page or shrink the sheet, do not raise the field")

pub data Map_Knuckles   = _map_knux
pub data DPLC_Knuckles  = _dplc_knux
pub data Art_Knuckles   = _art_knux
pub data Pal_SonicTails = _pal_sonic_tails
pub data Pal_Knuckles   = _pal_knux
